  "articleBody": "Dealing with roof damage can feel overwhelming, especially after one of Texas's notorious storms. But understanding what a professional roof damage evaluation involves can really demystify the process and help you make informed decisions. Let's break down what to expect.\n\n### Process Clarity\n\nAn evaluation starts with a thorough, ground-level visual review. This initial look helps identify obvious issues and ensures safety before anyone steps on the roof. Your evaluator should document everything – the date, any recent weather events, all sections of the roof, penetrations like chimneys or vents, gutters, and any signs of damage in your attic or interior. Photos are super important here; they provide clear evidence of what's going on.\n\nNext, trained professionals, using proper safety equipment, will carefully inspect the roof surface. They're looking for things like missing or cracked shingles, dents from hail, soft spots that indicate water damage, or issues with flashing around vents and chimneys. They'll distinguish between new storm damage and older wear and tear, which is a common mistake homeowners make. They’ll also check if damage is limited to the surface or if it extends to underlayment, decking, or insulation.\n\n**What to Prepare Before You Start**\n\nBefore your evaluation, it's helpful to have a few things ready. Think about when your roof was last inspected or repaired, if you've noticed any leaks inside, or if there have been any significant weather events recently. Having this information can help your evaluator understand the history of your roof. Make sure the area around your house is clear and accessible for the inspection crew. Also, be prepared to discuss any concerns you have, even if they seem minor. Every bit of information helps ensure a comprehensive assessment. Remember, the goal is to identify potential issues before they become costly repairs, giving you practical solutions tailored to your roof’s specific needs.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-03"
}